---
import BlocksGallery from '../components/BlocksGallery.vue';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

// The gallery's own first screen. It runs in the GPUI gallery under the title
// the story declares; the Slint gallery has no equivalent page, so `slug` is
// empty and the frame says so rather than loading a 404.
const welcome = [
  {
    id: 'introduction',
    title: 'The gallery',
    description:
      'Every component in one running application. This is what `cargo run` opens, and the fastest way to see how a view is put together before writing one.',
    story: 'Introduction',
    slug: '',
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Starter — GPUI Kit"
  description="A project to start from: the smallest working setup, and the paths out of it."
>
  <h1>Starter</h1>
  <p>
    The shortest path from an empty directory to a running window, and the ways
    out of it once the window is not enough. Everything below is in this
    repository and runs as written.
  </p>

  <BlocksGallery client:load blocks={welcome} baseUrl={import.meta.env.BASE_URL} />

  <h2>Start from the repository</h2>
  <p>
    Clone it and run the smallest example. It opens one window with a handful of
    components and no application structure to read around, which makes it the
    fastest way to see how a GPUI Kit view is put together.
  </p>
  <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/MohsenDastaran/uni-kit
cd uni-kit
cargo run -p hello_world</code></pre>
  <p>
    Each example is its own crate, so <code>-p</code> names the one you want.
    <code>cargo run</code> with no package runs the component gallery — the
    application shown above.
  </p>

  <h2>Start in your own crate</h2>
  <p>
    Add the umbrella crate and it brings the layers with it — the styled
    components, the base behaviour, and the default icon assets — along with a
    pinned GPUI underneath, so an application depends on one package rather than
    assembling a set of them.
  </p>
  <pre><code>[dependencies]
gpui-kit = "0.7"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Then call <code>gpui_component::init(cx)</code> before any component is used,
    and make the first view of a window a <code>Root</code>. The
    <a href="/docs/installation">installation guide</a> walks through both, and
    <a href="/docs/getting-started">getting started</a> takes it from there.
  </p>

  <h2>Take components rather than the whole crate</h2>
  <p>
    If you already have an application and want one control in it, copy the
    component in instead of depending on the library. The registry writes the
    component and everything it imports — including the icons it names — into
    your own source tree, where you can change them:
  </p>
  <pre><code>npx @dastaran/uni-kit add slint alert-dialog
npx @dastaran/uni-kit add slint table</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>--dir</code> overrides where the files land. This trades the ability to
    upgrade for the ability to edit, which is the point.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/installation">Installation</a> — toolchain setup for each platform.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a> — the first window, step by step.</li>
    <li><a href="/blocks">Blocks</a> — composed screens, once you need more than one control.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/design-guides">Design guides</a> — the conventions the components follow.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/coding-guides">Coding guides</a> — how to write against the library.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
